.TH "IMPLANTISOMD5" "1"
.SH "NAME"
implantisomd5 \(em implant an MD5 checksum in an ISO9660 image
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.PP
\fBimplantisomd5\fR [\fB\-\-force\fP]  [\fB\-\-supported-iso\fP]  [isofilename]
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
.PP
This manual page documents briefly the \fBimplantisomd5\fR command. \fBimplantisomd5\fR is a program that embeds an MD5 checksum in an unused section of an ISO9660 (.iso) image.  This checksum can later be compared to the .iso, or a block device, using the corresponding \fBcheckisomd5\fR command.
.SH "OPTIONS"
.IP "\fB\-\-force\fP" 10
Force an existing checksum to be overwritten.
.IP "\fB\-\-supported-iso\fP" 10
Indicate that the image will be written to a "supported" media, such as pressed CD.  On Red Hat-based Anaconda installers, this bypasses the prompt to check the CD.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.PP
Program returns exit status 0 if the checksum was implanted successfully, or 1 if an error occurred (for example, the file was not found, is not a valid ISO9660 image, or already contains a checksum and \fB\-\-force\fP was not specified).
.SH "EXAMPLES"
.TP
Implant an MD5 checksum into an ISO image:
.B implantisomd5 myimage.iso
.TP
Overwrite an existing checksum:
.B implantisomd5 --force myimage.iso
.TP
Mark the image as supported media:
.B implantisomd5 --supported-iso myimage.iso
.SH "FILES"
.TP
The checksum is stored in the application data area of the ISO9660 Primary Volume Descriptor (bytes 883\(en1395), which is unused by the ISO9660 specification and therefore safe to write.
.SH "BUGS"
.PP
Report bugs at \fIhttps://github.com/rhinstaller/isomd5sum/issues\fR or via the mailing list at \fIanaconda\-devel\-list@redhat.com\fR.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.PP
checkisomd5 (1), implantisosha (1), checkisosha (1).
